@node Timely Dataflow Model
@section Dataflow, timestamps and progress

Three ideas underlie every timely dataflow program: dataflow, logical
timestamps, and progress tracking.  This section takes the reactive
example of the previous section apart to show each.

@subsection Dataflow

Dataflow programming describes a program as independent components, each
acting in response to the availability of input data, plus the
connections between them.  In the reactive example the components are
the input, the @code{exchange}, the @code{inspect} and the probe; the
stream handles returned by each operator are the connections.  Nothing
in that program requires the workers to print in any particular order:
if you run it on two workers with the probe removed, the output arrives
in whatever order the workers happen to produce it.

That is only a mess if order matters, and often it does not.  If instead
of printing we filtered for prime numbers, the result would be the same
primes regardless of the order the records were examined in.  A
@code{filter} that keeps the primes is an operator like any other:

@example
static bool
is_prime(void *ctx, const void *rec, size_t len)
@{
        uint64_t x = dtfl_get_u64(rec), i, limit = (uint64_t)sqrt((double)x);

        if (x <= 1)
                return false;
        for (i = 2; i <= limit; i++)
                if (x % i == 0)
                        return false;
        return true;
@}
/* dtfl_stream_filter(s, is_prime, DTFL_NOCTX); */
@end example

@subsection Logical timestamps

When data move around arbitrarily, it becomes hard to correlate outputs
with inputs.  The standard fix is a @emph{timestamp} on each record: a
logical time that says @emph{when} the record should be thought to
happen.  A timestamp need not be a wall-clock time; it is often just a
sequence number identifying a batch of input.  As long as the
computation does not change the timestamps, a record introduced at time
@var{t} produces outputs at time @var{t}, so the output can always be
put back in order.

In the reactive example, each record carried the timestamp @code{round}.
The @code{inspect} callback does not see the time, but
@code{inspect_batch} does: it is called once per batch of records that
share a time, with that time:

@example
static void
hello_at(void *ctx, const uint64_t *time, const dtfl_message *msg)
@{
        size_t i, n = dtfl_message_len(msg);

        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) @{
                size_t len;
                const void *rec = dtfl_message_record(msg, i, &len);

                printf("hello %llu @ %llu\n",
                    (unsigned long long)dtfl_get_u64(rec),
                    (unsigned long long)time[0]);
        @}
@}
/* dtfl_stream_inspect_batch(s, hello_at, DTFL_NOCTX); */
@end example

Timestamps help operators too, not just users.  An operator that reports
a running sum @emph{as of} a timestamp cannot simply add records as they
arrive, because they may arrive out of timestamp order; it must
incorporate them in timestamp order.  To do that safely it needs to know
when a timestamp is complete, which is what progress tracking provides.

@subsection Tracking progress

Progress tracking lets each component learn how much of its input it has
seen.  Two methods on the input and the probe do the work:

@example
dtfl_input_advance_to(input, &next_epoch);
while (dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_time(input)))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
@end example

@code{dtfl_input_advance_to} is the important one.  An input has a
timestamp; it may introduce data at that time or later.  Advancing the
timestamp permanently restricts future data to times at or after the new
one, and that is the moment the program tells the system, and through it
every other worker, that a timestamp may soon be complete.  There may
still be records of that time in flight, but as they drain the system
can report progress.

A @dfn{probe} is how a program observes that progress.
@code{dtfl_probe_less_than} asks whether it is still possible to see a
time less than the argument at the probed point in the graph;
@code{dtfl_probe_less_equal} is the inclusive form.  Both are
non-blocking: they return immediately with a definite yes or no.  A
probe placed after @code{inspect} tells the program whether that
@code{inspect} might fire again for a given time; when the answer is no,
the program is free to advance the input and introduce the next round.

Progress information is passive.  A program observes what has happened
and may change its behavior, but it does not direct the system.  This
keeps coordination cheap: a worker that wants to wait for progress may,
but it may also go work on another part of the graph.  Synchronization
is a choice a worker makes, not a discipline the system imposes.
